.\" Written by hand: unrars parses unrar's command line without clap, so
.\" crates/rars-manpages cannot generate this page. Keep it to the commands
.\" and switches that parse() in crates/rars-cli/src/unrars.rs accepts.
.TH unrars 1 "" rars ""
.SH NAME
unrars \- extract, test and list RAR archives with unrar's command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unrars
.I command
.RI [ \-switch ...]
.I archive
.RI [ file ...]
.RI [ @listfile ...]
.RI [ path_to_extract/ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B unrars
reads RAR archives through the rars library and takes the part of
.BR unrar 's
command line that other programs call: the same commands, switches,
output lines and exit codes.
It extracts, tests, prints and lists; it does not create or change archives.
For that, and for options spelt the usual way, see
.BR rars (1).
.PP
Commands and switches are not case sensitive.
Switches start with
.B \-
and may come anywhere on the line, before or after the archive; an argument
.B \-\-
ends them, and every word after it is a command, archive, file or path.
.PP
The
.I archive
may hold the wildcards
.B *
and
.BR ? ,
which match archives in its directory.
A multi\-volume set is read from whichever of its volumes is named.
.PP
Each
.I file
is a mask that selects members.
A mask without wildcards is a path from the archive root, and selects a
directory together with what it holds.
A mask with wildcards and no directory is matched against each member's own
name, in any directory; with a directory it is matched against the whole path,
and its
.B *
reaches into subdirectories.
.BI @ listfile
reads masks from a file, one to a line.
With no masks, every member is selected.
.PP
The last word is where to extract to if it ends in a path separator or, when
extracting, if it names a directory that already exists.
Otherwise members are extracted into the current directory.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B x
Extract with full paths.
.TP
.B e
Extract every file straight into the destination, without its path.
.TP
.B t
Test archive files: decode them and check their checksums, writing nothing.
.TP
.B p
Print files to standard output.
.TP
.B l
List archive contents: attributes, size, date and name.
.TP
.B v
List archive contents with packed size, ratio and checksum as well.
.TP
.BR lt ", " vt
List a block of fields for each member.
.TP
.BR lta ", " vta
As
.BR lt ,
with service blocks too.
.TP
.BR lb ", " vb
List bare member names, one to a line.
.SH SWITCHES
.TP
.B \-?
Print the usage text and exit.
.TP
.B \-o+
Overwrite existing files.
.TP
.B \-o\-
Keep existing files.
.TP
.B \-or
Write beside an existing file as
.IR name ( N ). ext
rather than overwrite it.
.TP
.B \-y
Overwrite existing files, unless
.B \-o\-
or
.B \-or
was given.
Without any of these, unrars asks before overwriting, and keeps existing files
when there is no terminal to ask on.
.TP
.BI \-p password
Use this password.
.TP
.B \-p
Ask for the password before starting.
.TP
.B \-p\-
Do not ask for a password.
.IP
Without
.B \-p
an archive that needs a password asks for one on the terminal.
.TP
.B \-kb
Keep broken extracted files, with what could be read of them, and read past
damaged headers.
.TP
.B \-ver
Extract older file versions too.
.TP
.B \-v
With
.B l
and its kin, list every volume of a set, not only the one named.
.TP
.B \-vp
Ask before each later volume, and wait for the answer on standard input.
.TP
.BI \-x mask
Exclude members matching
.IR mask .
.BI \-x@ listfile
reads the masks from a file.
May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-n mask
Include only members matching
.IR mask .
.BI \-n@ listfile
reads the masks from a file.
May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-ap path
Extract the members under
.I path
in the archive without it.
Members outside it are extracted where they are.
.TP
.B \-ad
Extract into a directory named after the archive, under the destination.
.TP
.B \-ep
Extract without paths, as
.B e
does.
.TP
.BI \-op path
Extract into
.IR path .
.TP
.B \-ol\-
Leave symbolic links out.
.TP
.B \-ai
Ignore file attributes: set no file or directory modes.
.TP
.B \-ierr
Send all messages to standard error.
.TP
.B \-inul
Print no messages.
.TP
.B \-id\fR[\fBc\fR][\fBd\fR][\fBp\fR][\fBq\fR]
Turn messages off:
.B c
the banner,
.B d
the closing
.RB \(lq "All OK" \(rq,
.B p
the percentage on each member's line;
.B q
prints errors only.
The letters combine, as in
.BR \-idcdp .
.SS Refused switches
Switches that would change which files are written, or where, in ways unrars
does not support are refused with exit code 7, rather than ignored:
.TP
.BR \-ta ", " \-tb ", " \-tn ", " \-to ", " \-sl ", " \-sm ", " \-e "\fIattr\fR, " \-f ", " \-u
Filters by time, size, attributes or freshness.
.TP
.BR \-cl ", " \-cu
Case changes to the names written.
.TP
.BR \-ad1 ", " \-ad2 ", " \-ep1 " to " \-ep4 ", " \-ola
Absolute paths and links.
.PP
Other
.B unrar
switches are accepted and ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Warning, such as an archive that ends early.
.TP
.B 2
Fatal error.
.TP
.B 3
A checksum did not match, headers are damaged, or an encrypted member of a RAR 1.5 to 4.x archive could not be decoded.
.TP
.B 6
A volume of a multi\-volume set is missing.
.TP
.B 7
Invalid command line, or a refused switch.
.TP
.B 10
No files were selected.
.TP
.B 11
Wrong password.
.TP
.B 12
Standard input closed while unrars was asking on it.
.TP
.B 141
Whatever was reading standard output closed it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rars (1),
.BR unrar (1)
